<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.12.2: Transformations with Quadratic Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01946</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.12.2: Transformations with Quadratic Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4027a58e-da39-4701-af23-8efea991f74c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph \(f(x)=x^2\), and then experiment with each of the changes to the function in questions 1 – 7. Record your observations of what you noticed or wondered about each set of changes to the function (include sketches, if helpful).</para>


<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Add different constant terms to \(x^2\) (for example: \(x^2+5\), \(x^2+10\), \(x^2-3\)).</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> Changing the constant term either raised the graph (when the constant is positive) or lowered the graph (when the constant is negative). Adding the constant term shifted the graph vertically, but the overall shape of the graph did not change.</para>
</solution></exercise>

</example>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Multiply \(x^2\) by different positive coefficients greater than 1 (for example: \(3x^2\), \(7.5x^2\)).</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Multiplying \(x^2\) by different positive numbers changed the shape of the graph, making it "steeper" when the constant is larger and less steep when the constant is smaller. The size of the coefficient determined how much of a vertical stretch was applied to the graph.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Multiply \(x^2\) by different negative coefficients less than or equal to -1 (for example: \(-x^2\),\(-4x^2\)).</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>so</emphasis><emphasis>lution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Multiplying \(x^2\) by a negative number flipped the graph (or reflected the graph) over the \(x\)-axis so that it opens downward rather than upward.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Multiply \(x^2\) by different coefficients between -1 and 1 (for example: \(12x^2\), \(-0.25x^2\)).</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Multiplying \(x^2\) by numbers between -1 and 1 changed the opening of the graph as well as the direction of the graph. Shrinking the coefficient from 1 toward 0 made the graph "shallower" and wider. This change vertically compressed the graph. When the coefficient is 0, the graph becomes a horizontal line. Raising the coefficient from -1 to 0 had the same effect of vertically compressing the graph, except that the graph started out opening downward.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Add different constants to \(x\) before squaring the term (for example \((x-1)^2\), \((x+2)^2\), \((x-3)^2\)).</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>s</emphasis><emphasis>olution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>When a constant is added to the \(x\) in \(x^2\), the graph moves horizontally to the left. When a constant is subtracted from the \(x\) in \(x^2\), the graph moves horizontally to the right.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Multiply \(x\) by different positive coefficients greater than 1 (for example: (\(3x)^2\), \((7.5x)^2\)).</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Multiplying \(x\) by different positive numbers changed the shape of the graph, making it more narrow when the constant is larger and less narrow when the constant is smaller.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Multiply \(x\) by different positive coefficients between 0 and 1 (for example: \((0.5x)^2\), \((0.1x)^2\)).</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>s</emphasis><emphasis>olution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Multiplying \(x\) by different positive numbers between 0 and 1 changed the shape of the graph, making it wider as the decimal gets closer to zero.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><para>For quadratics, the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">parent function</term> is \(f(x)=x^2\) . From there, any moves created by making changes to the function like in questions 1 – 7 above are called <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">transformations</term>.</para>

